 "term_meanings": "from tools/device_fit.py (docstring and bound_source) and the cards' physics_defaults.how: kb bone-path scale, s scatter fraction, sigma_s scatter kernel, sigma_r receptor blur, alpha unsharp-mask (edge-enhancement) gain, sigma_u unsharp radius, b radial vignette, c anode-heel gradient, d cranio-caudal tilt gradient. tables/tabS1_device_terms.tex names alpha/sigma_u/b/c/d 'noise gain', 'noise blur', 'display slope', 'display offset', 'display gamma': those names are wrong (flagged to the manuscript owner; tools/make_supp_device_table.py TERM_DESC)",
 "refit_history": "docs/RESUME_2026-09-22_PM.md, checkpoint 19:50: 'A32 CLOSED NEGATIVE: every refit fails its own gate; the published cards stand.' Why: with the quantum draw inside the loss, least squares compares two independent noise realisations and smoothing always lowers it; the constrained fits drove sigma_r to the box edge (3-4) and alpha to 2.0 and n_e stayed 0. The values drawn are the refit files' own, not the resume's quotation of them (they agree).",
 "note_on_d_scale": "the refit's held-out R\u00b2 is under its own noisy, statistic-aware evaluation on 35-40 held-out frames, so the published card's value there (Cios 0.890, Elekta 0.972, Surgio 0.560) differs from the card's median held-out R\u00b2 in Table S1 (0.905, 0.974; Surgio-B51 has no Table S1 column)",
 "not_shown_and_why": {
  "Surgio-B51 and Varian cards in (a)-(c)": "Table S1 and the 24-fold card analysis (a3.json) cover the four devices drawn; the Surgio-B51 card (fitted on 40 C-ArmLSpine frames, held-out R\u00b2 0.584 vs 0.525) and the Varian card (0.446 vs 0.448, no gain) exist on the grid (docs/devices/) and are not in either; Surgio-B51 appears in (d) because the refit was run on it",
